bad-ips (4.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Role mode, for a fleet behind one internet edge. Set
    "role = block | report | off" and the 4.0 engine runs instead of the
    3.x daemon (which is unchanged, and still runs when no role is set):
    report hosts watch their logs and report to the shared ban list;
    the block host reads the whole list and drops it on the interfaces in
    block_interfaces only; off installs the package switched off.
  * New rule engine ([rule:NAME] sections, shipped in badips.d/60-rules.conf):
    every rule counts repeats (count within window) measured by the log
    line's OWN timestamp, takes the client address from a fixed place in
    the line via (?<ip>...), and bans for its own length. Rules merge by
    name across files, so a later file can replace or switch off a shipped
    rule (3.x could not). Shipped rules: nginx-probe, nginx-catchall-flood,
    postfix-auth, postfix-auth-slow, postfix-abuse, dovecot-auth.
  * Log sources resume from a saved inode/offset (or journal cursor), read
    back at most backread_bytes on a fresh start, and follow rotation by
    rename or truncation.
  * Never-block: a built-in floor (private, loopback, link-local, multicast)
    plus never_block_cidrs (IPv4 and IPv6, now both checked in code). Any
    entry that OVERLAPS a never-block range, in either direction, is refused
    at report time and again at block time; never-block is also written as
    fixed elements into 99-badips.nft, so an nftables reload restores it in
    the same step as the table.
  * The block role reconciles every reconcile_interval: it rebuilds the
    table in one atomic transaction when it is missing or wrong, otherwise
    applies only the differences; bans survive nftables reloads; a starting
    or rotated-in node loads every active ban with its real end time; DNS
    (port 53) is accepted ahead of the drops (dns_accept).
  * Shared list: detections are reported directly (not only after a local
    block), feed and pulled entries are never re-shared, a re-ban keeps the
    later end time, and a database outage only delays sharing (bounded
    queue, retry with backoff) instead of stopping it until a reload.
    New exemptions table; schema in /usr/local/share/bad_ips/schema.sql.
  * New commands: bad_ips unban | ban | list | why | test-rules, and a
    role-mode --test-config that also checks the database is reachable.
  * bad_ips_nft: new state / sync / render commands; sync validates a whole
    desired state, checks it with nft -c and applies it as one transaction.
  * [feed:NAME] public blocklists for the block role (Spamhaus DROP v4 and
    v6 shipped; EDROP was merged into DROP), cached, sanity-checked, local.
  * prerm no longer disables the service on upgrade.
  * Test suite (t/, run with prove, also at package build).

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 16:25:49 -0600

bad-ips (3.8.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * GeneralLogMonitor: recognise successful logins. New
    good_login_pattern_NN keys mark log lines that prove a real user
    authenticated. They are tested before the bad patterns and win outright,
    and the matching address is then exempt from blocking for
    good_login_grace seconds (default 3600) with any accumulated failure
    tally discarded. Previously the daemon had no notion of a successful
    login at all: a key-based login followed seconds later by a harmless
    pre-auth disconnect was enough to block the legitimate user.
  * GeneralLogMonitor: honour block_after_n_attempts. The key was parsed and
    documented but never read by any code, so a single pattern match always
    blocked immediately. Matches are now counted per address over the new
    attempt_window (default 600 seconds).
  * GeneralLogMonitor: extract only the client address from a matching line.
    The previous global match harvested every address on the line, so any
    line naming more than one host could block innocent bystanders.
    Addresses are now located by position relative to a known anchor
    (rhost=, rip=, client:, "from", "closed/reset by", bracketed forms),
    falling back to the first address rather than all of them.
  * 10-sshd.conf: stop matching bare "Connection closed by" and "Connection
    reset by" pre-auth disconnects. These are ordinary client behaviour, not
    attacks, and matching them locked out real users. The "Connection closed
    by authenticating user" variant is kept, since it names a username.
  * 10-sshd.conf: escape the parentheses in the pam_unix(sshd:auth) pattern.
    Unescaped they formed a capture group, so the rule silently never
    matched a real PAM line. Restore the "no matching key exchange method
    found" rule, which had been lost to a duplicated bad_login_pattern_08
    key. Default threshold is now 3 matches in 600 seconds.
  * Document good_login_pattern_NN, good_login_grace and attempt_window in
    the man page and CONFIGURATION.md, correct the stale SSH detector
    example, and note that per-plugin block_duration is not implemented.
  * Sync the stale VERSION file and DEBIAN/control with debian/changelog;
    they still read 3.6.1 while the package was built as 3.7.0, so the
    installed /usr/share/doc/bad-ips/VERSION disagreed with dpkg.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sat, 03 Oct 2026 10:57:21 -0500

